  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 200 PM MDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nA big pattern change is still on track for mid to late week as an \namplified ridge over the Intermountain West is replaced by a digging \ntrough and closed low. After spending a couple days over the Pac NW \nand Great Basin, the low rounds the Four Corners region and emerges \nonto the Plains near the WY/NE/CO point Wednesday into Thursday. A \n100+ kt H3 jet streak, a stout mid-level shortwave, and a surface \ncool front all precede the low's passage. With moisture streaming \nnortheastward throughout this process, widespread precipitation and \nmuch cooler temperatures are in order.\n\nTuesday... Considerable mid-upper level moisture will be in place as \nthe core of the jet passes nearby, and east/southeast H85 flow helps \nwith orographic forcing. Temperatures should hold warm enough \n(plains/valleys highs around 60F) and snow levels high enough \n(10kft) to result in rain as the dominant p-type throughout the day. \nIn fact, relatively steep mid-level lapse rates and marginal MUCAPE \nacross southern WY may be sufficient for a few rumbles of thunder. \n\nWednesday... The H5 low is knocking on our doorstep as the H7 0C \ntherm presses east across WY. Near surface flow takes on a more \nnortheasterly direction, and forcing strengthens at multiple levels. \nThe NBM envelope has trended cooler considerably, now suggesting 40s \nfor highs in WY and lower 50s for NE. Forecast snow levels drop \nbelow 9kft by the evening time, squarely placing the Sierra Madres \nand Snowy Range in the winter regime. At this time, snow totals \nappear to hold right near advisory criteria for the higher peaks. \nWill need to monitor any further cooling trend as the event draws \nnearer however, if snow levels drop or more time is spent in the \ndendritic growth zone. Rainfall outside the ranges could be \nsignificant as well, with some locations approaching 1\" of moisture. \nWed night may necessitate frost/freeze headlines, especially for WY \nand northern panhandle zones. The median of the NBM envelope \nsuggests upper 20s west for most of Carbon and Albany Cos, lower/mid \n30s for I-25 corridor and into Sioux Co. \n\nThursday... Precip tapers across the forecast area, but winds really \npick up as north/northwest flow fully takes over. 30+ kts flow at H5-\n7 should easily translate to the surface via mechanical mixing, and \nheight gradients surpass 30m across WY/CO. Temperatures attempt to \nrebound with gradual clearing through the day, with highs generally \nin the 50s (WY) and lower 60s (NE).  \n\nFriday and beyond... The upper ridge rebuilds over the Western US, \nand a broad surface high settles onto the Great Plains. Seasonably \nmild and dry conditions come right back next weekend, with highs in \nthe 70s near/east of I-25 for Saturday and Sunday.",
